<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sansepolcro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Borgo Sansepolcro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sansepolcro, formerly Borgo Sansepolcro, is a town in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> valley in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, Tuscany. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> sold it to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1440, a transaction whose loose wording left the tiny Republic of Cospaia unclaimed for centuries. It was the birthplace of the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a>, whose Resurrection hangs in its civic museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sansepolcro.jpg" alt="Sansepolcro" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro.puleri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sansepolcro_veduta_aerea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to tradition the town arose from two 9th-century pilgrims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, Arcanus and Giles. They returned, built a chapel dedicated to Saint Leonard and began a monastic life there. They had brought a stone from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which was installed in the monastery and gave the place its name, Santo Sepolcro.</p><p>It became a popular pilgrimage site, and the church was raised to the rank of Benedictine abbey, the Badia, and declared an abbey nullius. The first historical mentions date to 1012 and refer to the building of the monastery, around which a commune began to develop. The Emperor Conrad II declared the settlement a market town, and in the following century the abbey affiliated with the Camaldolese Order.</p><p>During the conflicts of Guelfs and Ghibellines the town's factions were led by local families, among them the Pichi, Bercordati, Graziani and Bacci. In the 13th century, because of its position on local trade routes, control of the town passed between Uguccione della Faggiola, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guido-tarlati">Guido Tarlati</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, and his brother Pier Saccone Tarlati di Pietramala.</p><p>In 1367 Pope Urban V gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">Malatesta family</a>, whose heirs ruled it until the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> took control in the 15th century with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a>. Pope Leo X raised it to the rank of a city a century later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sansepolcro stands on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, in eastern Tuscany. Its Cathedral of St. John the Evangelist, in Gothic-Romanesque style, was built in 1012-49. It was the birthplace of the painters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a>, Raffaellino del Colle, Matteo di Giovanni, Santi di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and Angiolo Tricca, and of the mathematician Luca Pacioli. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the town was saved from destruction when a British officer, Tony Clarke, halted an Allied artillery attack to protect Piero della Francesca's fresco of the Resurrection. That painting is now in the Museo Civico.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sansepolcro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1440 Pope Eugene IV sold <b>Borgo Sansepolcro</b> to the Republic of Florence.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cospaia">Republic of Cospaia</a></li></ul></section>
